{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>For I have known him.</b> This is language of greatness and excellence, in the manner of (Psalms 144:3) ״מה אדם ותדעהו״; or it may be language of providence, for the knowledge of HaShem, may He be exalted, of a person is His providence over him; and when He said 'for I have known him,' it comes to diminish the rest of mankind who are not righteous, in whom the providence is not as it is upon the righteous." "<b>And you must</b> understand that providence in the lower world over the human species is both general and particular, and this is a full verse, for we find (Jeremiah 32:19): ״גדול העצה ורב העליליה אשר עיניך פקוחות על כל דרכי בני האדם לתת לאיש כדרכיו״ — 'great in counsel and mighty in deed, whose eyes are open upon all the ways of the children of men, to give every man according to his ways'; and upon the other living beings it is general, not particular, only in general to maintain the species. And the particular providence over the human species is divided into two parts: providence over him to know all the details of his deeds and thoughts, and providence over him to protect him and deliver him from accidents. The providence over him to know all the details of his deeds applies to every person, whether of Israel or of the nations, as it is written (Psalms 33:15): ״היוצר יחד לבם המבין אל כל מעשיהם״ — 'He who fashions their hearts together, who understands all their deeds'; the providence over him to deliver him from accidents is not upon every person, not even upon all of Israel, but only upon the righteous among them — for the Holy One, blessed be He, delivers the righteous from the accidents to which the rest of mankind are delivered over, and He does not forsake His devout ones nor remove His eye from them, but His providence over the righteous never departs from them at all. And this is the sense of 'for I have known him' — that the providence is upon him and upon the rest of the righteous to deliver them from the accidents of mankind. And this is the opinion of the Ramban on this verse when you examine it." "And the phrase <b>'for I have known him'</b> constitutes words of YHWH becoming known to the fathers — that He is the One who exercises providence and bestows good upon Abraham, so that he will command his seed after him to keep the way of YHWH: He is YHWH, who appeared to them as El Shaddai, which had not been known to them. And this is clear." "<b>In order that he may command his children and his household.</b> Our Rabbi Hananel, of blessed memory, wrote: the words of the fathers became tradition without being written in the Torah, just like the written Torah; and from here you learn that the Mishnah, which is arranged as the telling of fathers to children, is a tradition whose words one is obligated to keep just as one is obligated to keep the words of the written Torah, for these and those are equal — for the Ten Commandments were heard from the mouth of the Almighty and written by the mouth of the Prophet, peace be upon him, and the rest of the Torah was written by the mouth of the Prophet; and just as there is no difference between commandments that are by hearing and commandments that are by writing, so there is no difference between commandments that are by writing and commandments that are by tradition, for all are equal — end of quote."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :register {:flags {3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:32.151898174Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}